  "status": "UNSAT_for_this_degree_sequence",
  "lemma": "In R=22/24 low-graph boundary, no degree-11 vertex meets a degree-13/14 vertex. At an extreme degree-13 vertex S=164, k of its neighbors are degree 14, and its induced high degree is 8-k. Its 2+k high nonneighbors must lie among vertices completely anticomplete to the six low vertices in its B88 nonneighborhood; B88 scans allow at most 3, forcing k<=1. k=0 would require eight degree-13 neighbors, but only seven exist. Thus k=1. With D=8*164-S13, at least 8-D of the eight degree-13 vertices are extreme. Hence e13_14<=8+2D, or e13_14<=8+D when the three degree-14 vertices form a triangle. Every class matrix violates these necessary bounds.",
  "notes": "The R=24 low graph has zero degree-profile candidates in every B88, independently excluding those configurations. Degree-profile superset scan bounds max anticomplete count, and NetworkX induced-isomorphism confirms the exact counts. No withdrawn row-CSP or SAT.",
  "validation": "Python exhaustive graph6 six-subset scan and independent C++ bitset graph6 decoder; class-handshake enumeration cross-checked in Python/C++."
}
